+# Signet SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s block signatures (`-signetslhblock`)
+
+A custom signet that already requires an ML-DSA-44 block signature
+(`-signetpqblock`, [signet-pqblock.md](signet-pqblock.md)) can require, from a
+height on, a second one: an SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s (FIPS 205) signature by a fixed
+public key, in a coinbase output of its own. Nodes without the rule keep
+accepting those blocks: the rule only narrows what is valid.
+
+This is an experiment of the Ark-0 test network (M4-B of its M4 design). It is
+not part of BIP 325 or any other BIP. **It is not active on the Ark-0 signet**:
+its height and key there are not decided, `ARK0_SLH_BLOCK_HEIGHT` is
+`std::numeric_limits<int>::max()` and the key is unset, so an Ark-0 node has no
+such rule and refuses the option. Both are set in one later commit.
+
+## What it proves and what it does not
+
+From the height on, a node with the rule accepts a block only if three
+signatures hold together: the signet solution, the ML-DSA-44 block signature
+and the SLH-DSA block signature. The SLH-DSA signature covers what the ML-DSA-44
+one covers, less the SLH signature itself: the version, the previous block, the
+time, every transaction, and the whole coinbase except the ML-DSA-44 push, the
+signet solution push and the SLH signature. If lattice signatures fail, the
+ML-DSA-44 signature is still required but the authorization rests on SLH-DSA
+and the classical key; if hash-based signatures fail, the other way round.
+
+It does not make anything else post-quantum. Like the other two, it does not
+cover `nNonce` or `nBits`. Nodes without the rule do not check it. One party
+holds all three keys.
+
+## Configuration
+
+    -signetslhblock=<pubkey>@<height>
+
+- `<pubkey>`: the 32-byte SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s public key, `PK.seed || PK.root`,
+  in hex.
+- `<height>`: at least 1, and at or above the `-signetpqblock` height.
+- It needs `-signetchallenge` and `-signetpqblock`; it is refused on the Ark-0
+  signet, on the default signet and on every other chain; it takes one value.
+
+It is a consensus rule of that network: every node must use the same value
+before the chain reaches `<height>`. A node logs at startup
+
+    Signet SLH-DSA block signature required from height <height>, public key SHA256 <hex>
+
+which operators compare.
+
+## The block
+
+Let `c` be the index of the witness commitment output: the last coinbase
+output whose script starts with `6a 24 aa 21 a9 ed` and has at least 38 bytes.
+From the height on (rule 4B-1):
+
+- `c >= 1`;
+- `vout[c - 1]` has amount 0 and a script of exactly 7,865 bytes, whose first
+  9 bytes are `6a 4d b5 1e 50 51 48 31 01`: `OP_RETURN`, `OP_PUSHDATA2` of
+  7,861 = 0x1eb5 bytes (the shortest push of that length), the header `PQH1`,
+  the version `01`, then the 7,856-byte signature.
+
+And (4B-2) no other coinbase output starts with `6a 4d b5 1e 50 51 48 31`.
+The node compares these bytes at fixed offsets; it never parses the scripts.
+
+The output is `OP_RETURN`: it is not in the UTXO set and counts no sigops. It
+adds 7,876 bytes of non-witness data, 31,504 weight units, to every block.
+
+The SLH output sits **before** the witness commitment output, which stays
+last: `contrib/signet/miner`'s `finish_block` puts the signet solution on the
+last output, and Core's own template code (`GenerateCoinbaseCommitment`,
+`RegenerateCommitments`) keeps the commitment last. Core's template code is
+not changed: `getblocktemplate` still has exactly one required output, and the
+producer inserts the SLH output itself.
+
+## The message
+
+    slh_coinbase = pq_coinbase with vout[c - 1]'s script replaced by 6a 05 50514831 01
+    slh_merkle   = ComputeMerkleRoot([txid(slh_coinbase)] + the other txids)
+    slh_msg      = TaggedHash("SignetSLHBlock",
+                              SHA256(challenge) || nVersion || hashPrevBlock || slh_merkle || nTime)
+
+`pq_coinbase` is the coinbase as the ML-DSA-44 signature covers it: the witness
+commitment output cut back to `OP_RETURN`, the commitment and the push
+`50514231 01` (`signet-pqblock.md`). The preimage is 104 bytes, in the byte
+order of a block header. The signature is FIPS 205 `slh_sign`, pure SLH-DSA,
+with the context string `SIGNET/SLHBLOCK/EXP0`: neither `OP_CHECKSLHDSA128S`'s
+nor the ML-DSA-44 block signature's.
+
+`src/test/data/signet_slhblock_p2mr_ts.json` holds three messages that p2mr-ts,
+a separate TypeScript implementation, computed; `signet_slhblock_tests` checks
+`slh_coinbase`, `slh_merkle` and `slh_msg` against them.
+
+## The order of the signatures
+
+Nodes without this rule compute the ML-DSA-44 message over the SLH output as it
+is, so the SLH signature has to be final before the ML-DSA-44 signature is made.
+A producer goes through four steps:
+
+1. From a template (with `blockreservedweight` at least 48,000, see below),
+   insert the placeholder `6a 05 50514831 01` before the witness commitment
+   output, whose index goes up by one, and append the cut ML-DSA-44 push
+   `05 50514231 01` to the commitment output. The coinbase is now
+   `slh_coinbase`.
+2. Compute `slh_msg`.
+3. Sign it with hedged SLH-DSA (fresh `opt_rand`); verify the signature with the
+   public key, and make no block if it fails.
+4. Put the full SLH output in place of the placeholder, take the cut push off,
+   sign the ML-DSA-44 message (which covers the SLH signature), add the
+   ML-DSA-44 push and recompute the merkle root; then the signet solution as
+   before (`generate_psbt`, `walletprocesspsbt`, `finish_block`).
+
+There is no circular dependency: `slh_msg` covers neither block signature nor
+the signet push; the ML-DSA-44 message covers the SLH signature but not itself
+or the signet push; the signet solution covers both block signatures; the
+witness commitment covers only the other transactions' wtxids. Signed the other
+way round (ML-DSA-44 first, SLH output inserted after), a block fails the
+ML-DSA-44 check (`bad-signet-pqblk`) on new and old nodes alike.
+
+`test/functional/test_framework/signet_slh.py` has these steps
+(`add_signet_slh_and_pq_signatures`); the Ark-0 producer calls the same
+functions.
+
+## The signer
+
+`signet-slh-sign` (`src/signet-slh-sign.c`, built with
+`-DBUILD_SIGNET_SLH_SIGNER=ON`, the default when tests are built; **Linux only**,
+as it relies on `prctl(PR_SET_DUMPABLE)` and `getrandom`; linked fully static and
+not position-independent, which needs `libc.a`, from `libc6-dev`) signs with the
+vendored slhdsa-c of `src/crypto/slhdsa128s/`, the library the node verifies
+with; no node binary links it.
+
+    signet-slh-sign <keyfile>            < 64 hex characters (slh_msg)  > signature hex
+    signet-slh-sign -pubkey <keyfile>    > public key hex
+
+`<keyfile>` holds exactly the 48 bytes `SK.seed || SK.prf || PK.seed` (FIPS 205
+`slh_keygen_internal`), raw. The program reads and checks the message before it
+opens the key file, signs with a 16-byte `opt_rand` from `getrandom`, verifies
+the signature with the public key, and only then prints it. It never prints
+the key.
+
+**Exit status.** Exit 0: standard output holds the result. Any other exit
+(1 usage, 2 input, 3 randomness, 4 signing or verification, 5 output,
+6 the process could not be made non-dumpable): **ignore standard output**. It
+is empty, except when writing the result itself failed part way, which can
+leave part of it there. The test framework's `program_slh_signer` and the
+Ark-0 producer read standard output only after exit 0.
+
+**The secret.**
+
+- Before the key file is opened, the process sets `PR_SET_DUMPABLE` to 0 and
+  checks it with `PR_GET_DUMPABLE`; if either fails it exits (6) without
+  touching the key. A non-dumpable process gets no core dump at all:
+  `do_coredump()` in the kernel's `fs/coredump.c` returns before anything else
+  when the dumpable flag is 0, whether `core_pattern` names a file, a pipe or a
+  socket. It also keeps unprivileged processes of the same user from ptrace and
+  from `/proc/<pid>/mem`.
+- The key file is read with `open()` and `read()` into the program's own
+  buffer, so no stdio buffer holds it.
+- Every buffer of the program's own that held the key, the expanded secret key
+  or `opt_rand` is overwritten on every return path. The vendored library is not
+  changed (the node compiles it too); instead, after key generation, signing
+  and verification, a non-inlined function overwrites 64 KiB of the stack below
+  the caller through volatile writes (as libgcrypt's `burn_stack`): the library
+  uses less than 9 KiB for each of the three (measured).
+- What remains is out of reach of a C program: CPU registers, and the
+  process's pages until the kernel reclaims them at exit. With dumps off and a
+  process that lives under a second, nothing else can read them.
+- **The guarantee, and what it rests on.** The stack scrub covers the
+  library's state (its `slh_var_t` copy of the key among it). Three mechanisms
+  defeat it, by moving stack frames where the scrub does not reach:
+  AddressSanitizer's fake stack (HWAddressSanitizer is refused with it),
+  SafeStack's unsafe stack (`-fsanitize=safe-stack`, which moves address-taken
+  locals such as the key's buffers) and GCC's split stacks (`-fsplit-stack`,
+  frames on segments from `__morestack`). The source, the configure check and
+  release staging refuse all three.
+  - The source does not compile under the sanitizers a compiler announces
+    (address, hwaddress, memory, thread, safe-stack). Neither GCC 13 nor
+    Clang 18 announces `-fsplit-stack` (no macro), so the source cannot see it.
+  - CMake builds no production `signet-slh-sign` when `SANITIZERS` is set, or
+    `-fsanitize` or `-fsplit-stack` appears in the compiler arguments
+    (`CMAKE_<LANG>_COMPILER_ARG1`), the final C, C++ and executable linker flags
+    (per configuration too; `CFLAGS` and `CXXFLAGS` included, as the decision is
+    taken where the targets are made, after every language is enabled), Core's
+    `APPEND_*` flags, or the options of the SLH library and
+    `sanitize_interface`; the configure output says which. This covers the
+    sanitizers that move no frames too (UBSan among them): no shipped signer
+    carries any. Only the test option `-DSIGNET_SLH_SIGN_ALLOW_SANITIZERS=ON`
+    builds a separate target with `SANITIZERS`,
+    `signet-slh-sign-sanitizer-test`, marked and with no install rule.
+  - Release staging (`check-signer.sh` in the patch repository) sees what a
+    compiler launcher or wrapper script added, as each mechanism links its own
+    runtime. The production `signet-slh-sign` is a **fully static, non-PIE
+    executable**: it loads no library and no loader, so there are no
+    dependencies to follow. The check verifies that, an x86-64 ELF64
+    little-endian `ET_EXEC` whose program headers and `PT_LOAD` segments lie
+    within the file and which has neither `PT_INTERP` nor `PT_DYNAMIC`, and
+    searches its one file for the test build's mark
+    `SIGNET_SLH_SIGN_SANITIZER_BUILD`, the fault build's switch, the runtimes'
+    symbol prefixes (`__asan_`, `__hwasan_`, `__ubsan_`, `__tsan_`, `__msan_`,
+    `__lsan_`, `__sanitizer_`, `__safestack_`, `__morestack`) and, as stripping
+    removes symbols, the strings the runtimes carry as data
+    (`AddressSanitizer`, `HWAddressSanitizer`, `ThreadSanitizer`,
+    `MemorySanitizer`, `LeakSanitizer`, `UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer`, `ubsan: `,
+    `safestack CHECK failed`, and libgcc's `SPLIT_STACK_GUARD`). The lists cover
+    the runtimes known in Clang 18 and GCC 13; the static release signer, glibc
+    included, holds none of these strings. **Its scope:** it stops a signer
+    from a mistaken build or configuration from going into the image; it is not
+    a defence against someone who controls the build host, nor against a
+    runtime the lists do not know.
+  - **That is defence in depth, not the guarantee.** A shipped signer comes
+    only from the release pipeline (`contrib/k3s/build.sh` in the patch
+    repository), with its pinned toolchain and flags, and the producer image's
+    digest is recorded in the runbook.
+
+`tool_signet_slh_sign.py` tests all of this: the interface; that the running
+signer is not dumpable (root owns its `/proc/<pid>/status` while it waits on
+standard input, as the kernel does for a non-dumpable process, where a control
+process of the same user owns its own); that the message comes before the key
+file; the test-only build `signet-slh-sign-fault-test`
+(`SIGNET_SLH_SIGN_TEST_BUILD`, never installed), whose faults
+(`SIGNET_SLH_SIGN_TEST_FAULT=flip`, a bit of every signature flipped before
+verification; `=random`, a failing random source) must print nothing; and that
+the framework kills a signer that hangs, ignores the output of a non-zero exit,
+and checks the signature against the public key it checked.
+
+A key is 48 bytes from the operating system's CSPRNG. It never belongs in a
+persistent file: make it under `umask 077` on a RAM-backed path such as
+`/dev/shm`, move it into the encrypted backups and the producer's secret store,
+and remove it. SLH-DSA is stateless: restoring a backup or switching machines
+reuses no one-time key.
+
+## Where it is checked
+
+At the end of `CheckBlock`, after the ML-DSA-44 block signature and before
+`fChecked` is set, on the same condition (`signet_blocks && fCheckPOW`):
+
+- the height is the one the coinbase commits to (BIP34); a height that cannot
+  be read counts as reached;
+- the genesis block is exempt by its hash;
+- first the form, then `slh_merkle`, then the signature, about 1 ms;
+- the reason is `bad-signet-slhblk`.
+
+Below the height nothing is rejected; with `-debug=validation` each block gets
+one line, `CheckSignetSLHBlock: block <hash> below height <h>, SLH-DSA block
+signature valid | invalid | absent or not in the required form`.
+
+Templates (`getblocktemplate`, `TestBlockValidity`, which checks without
+`fCheckPOW`) skip it, as they skip the other two signatures; `getblocktemplate`
+keeps working from the height on. It does not depend on the UTXO set or on any
+tripwire.
+
+## Block weight
+
+The SLH output is 31,504 WU, on top of the ML-DSA-44 push (about 9,720 WU) and
+the signet solution push. A producer's node must run with
+`-blockreservedweight` of at least 48,000 **before** the first block with an
+SLH output: with the old 16,000 a full template no longer fits once the output
+is in (`feature_signet_slhblock.py` shows both).
+
+## Nodes that upgrade late
+
+A node accepts blocks once; it does not check them again when it gets the rule.
+At startup `-checkblocks` (6 by default) checks the last blocks, and one
+without a valid SLH signature at or above the height stops the node with
+`Corrupted block database detected`. That covers only the last few blocks. A
+node that upgrades after the height must run `-reindex-chainstate` (a pruned
+node `-reindex`); a node with a manual `-assumevalid` drops it first.
